p/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D9/2/code.py:36:#   bottom-right; a red sprite placed in the corridor between those frames still contributes one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D9/2/code.py:52:# - That heuristic becomes riskier once frames are smaller and corridors are tighter, so frame\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D9/2/code.py:62:#   bounding box of all frames are free. Cells outside that corridor region are marked occupied.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D9/2/code.py:74:# - Sprites are confined to corridor background, so no object layering ambiguity occurs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D9/2/code.py:75:# - A tall horizontal corridor between the two frame rows gives enough room for sprites without\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D9/2/code.py:105:    2: [2, 16],      # 2x2 layout: wide middle corridor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f215267/D9/2/code.py:106:    3: [0, 9, 18],   # 2x3 layout: smaller but still visible vertical corridors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e3721c99/34/code.py:93:#    - `main()` assumes enough free room exists in the reserved strip for the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:3:# Change: 房间数相同时，被覆盖在下方的框架排在前面（底部优先）... → 按框架位置（行优先）消歧\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:19:# - Each real frame still maps to one horizontal output bar whose length equals its room count.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:20:# - Only the tie-breaker changes: if two frames have the same room count, the one whose bbox starts\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:24:# - Original task: two 3-room frames could appear in output order because one visibly sits under\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:27:# - Variant: the same two 3-room frames are compared by their top-left positions instead. A tied\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:28:#   pair can now be disjoint. Example: blue 3-room frame at (4, 14), red 3-room frame at (11, 3).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:34:#   tied room-count pair. Without that, many samples would be identical to the original task.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:40:# - Old main() assumed every equal-room group could be linearly ordered by visible overlap; that is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:116:def _room_count_for_color(g: np.ndarray, color: int) -> int:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:143:    Convert each colored partition frame into a horizontal bar whose length equals its enclosed-room count.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:148:    - Real objects may overlap, but each genuine drawing is still a rectangular wall layout enclosing one or more rooms.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:154:    - Sort true frames by room count ascending; when room counts tie, compare the visible bbox anchors in row-major order (topmost first, then leftmost).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:165:        rooms = _room_count_for_color(g, int(color))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:169:        counts.append((int(color), int(rooms), walls, (int(b[0]), int(b[1]))))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:171:    drawings = [(color, rooms, walls, anchor) for color, rooms, walls, anchor in counts if rooms > 0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:172:    pad_colors = [color for color, rooms, _walls, _anchor in counts if rooms == 0]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:178:    for rooms in sorted({rooms for _color, rooms, _walls, _anchor in drawings}):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:179:        group = [item for item in drawings if item[1] == rooms]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/89565ca0/D2/1/code.py:182:    width = max(rooms for _color, rooms, _walls, _a