{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed581286445/12/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"320afe60\" lines=\"41-56\">\n<description>\nSlide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.\n\nInput structure:\n- Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.\n- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n- Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.\n\nRule:\n- If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.\n- Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red.\n- Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.\n\nKey insight:\n- The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9f669b64\" lines=\"26-48\">\n<description>\nPush the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"412b6263\" lines=\"39-54\">\n<description>\nInfer the background, rotate the whole input 90 degrees counterclockwise, and stack two framed copies vertically.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue is absent from the input.\n- One rare marker color appears in exactly two pixels.\n- The background is the unique non-marker color whose removal leaves exactly two 4-connected foreground objects, each with one marker pixel and one body color.\n- The whole grid, including background and foreground objects, is the sprite to transform.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate the entire input grid 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Create a blue one-pixel frame around two vertical copies of the rotated grid.\n- Use the input background color for the two endpoints of every horizontal frame/separator row.\n\nKey insight: The output is not a crop of the foreground objects; it is two copies of the complete rotated input, separated and enclosed by a newly added blue border.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"15660dd6\" lines=\"33-54\">\n<description>\nCombine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.\n- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.\n- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,\n  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on\n  the background.\n\nRule:\n- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.\n- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.\n- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask\n  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one\n  background column.\n\nKey insight:\nThe table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies\nthe paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply\nthe shape mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"faa9f03d\" lines=\"36-52\">\n<description>\nRepair a one-cell-thick colored line network by replacing singleton markers, filling short orthogonal gaps, and redirecting yellow-marked arms to the edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A rectangular grid with a dominant background.\n- Line colors form one-cell-thick horizontal/vertical stroke fragments.\n- Marker colors are exactly those whose 4-connected components are all singleton pixels.\n- Red singleton markers are missing line cells; yellow singleton markers are directional controls next to a line endpoint.\n\nRule:\n- Assign every marker to the nearest visible line color in row/column line-of-sight, preferring an endpoint supported along that same axis.\n- For each line color, fill the gap between consecutive facing endpoints in a row or column.\n- A yellow control erases the old straight arm on the far side of its endpoint and paints the same color from that endpoint through the control pixel to the grid edge.\n- At crossings, yellow-control rays win; otherwise an original-vs-filled crossing uses the more fragmented pre-repair line color, while pure filled crossings use the lower color id.\n\nKey insight: The singleton markers identify which sparse stroke fragments belong together; after they are colored, the intended lines are the axis-aligned gaps between facing endpoints, with yellow controls flipping one arm to the marked side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"a32d8b75\" lines=\"262-275\">\n<description>\nAssemble and place enlarged sprites on a canvas using legend-panel instructions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Color-6 separators divide the grid into a left legend column and a main canvas.\n- Each legend panel has (top to bottom): a unit sprite, a frame template, a 5x5 bordered square with a position-marker pixel, and a 3x3 rotation clock-hand.\n\nRule:\n- Tile the unit sprite into each active cell of the frame to build an enlarged composite.\n- Rotate the composite per the clock-hand direction (up=0, right=90, down=180, left=270 degrees).\n- Place it on the canvas anchored at the position encoded by the marker pixel's row/col in the bordered square's 3x3 inner grid.\n\nKey insight: The marker pixel's position in a 3x3 grid jointly encodes vertical and horizontal anchor alignment (start/middle/end).\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7666fa5d\" lines=\"12-26\">\n<description>\nFill the parallelogram-shaped region between parallel anti-diagonal line segments with red.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One non-bg color forms short diagonal segments, each lying on a single anti-diagonal (r+c = const).\n- Segments come in parallel pairs bounding a region to be filled.\n\nRule:\n- For each main-diagonal axis (r−c = const), collect anti-diagonal segments whose span covers that axis.\n- Sort those segments by their anti-diagonal index (r+c).\n- Fill all bg cells lying strictly between consecutive segment pairs along that axis with red (color 2).\n\nKey insight: The two diagonal directions decouple — segments sit on anti-diagonals, and filling sweeps along main diagonals between them, forming parallelogram regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c920a713\" lines=\"87-102\">\n<description>\nUntangle overlapping colored rectangular frames into concentric square frames ordered by their visible crossings.\n\nInput structure\n- The background contains several one-cell-thick axis-aligned rectangular frames, one color per frame.\n- Frames overlap, and the visible color at an overlap records a directed crossing from the hidden frame to the visible frame.\n- Each foreground color still has visible pixels on the perimeter of its full rectangular bounding box.\n\nRule\n- Infer each color's full rectangular frame from the bounding box of its visible pixels.\n- Add a directed edge A -> B when B is visible at a crossing of A and B's frame perimeters.\n- Find the unique path that visits every frame color once, then render those colors as one-pixel-thick concentric square frames from outside to inside.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a crop of the input; the crossing graph gives a single frame order, and that order is redrawn as nested rings.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"20fb2937\" lines=\"63-77\">\n<description>\nReplace each marker below the separator with the mapped top legend square centered on that marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background surrounds a top legend, one solid non-background separator row, and a lower marker field.\n- The legend contains equal-size filled square sprites, with a singleton marker below each sprite and aligned to its center column.\n- The lower field contains only background and marker colors from the legend.\n\nRule:\n- Pair legend markers to legend sprites by left-to-right position.\n- Discard the legend and separator; keep the grid width and the rows below the separator.\n- In the lower field, replace every marker pixel by a filled square of the paired sprite color, centered at the marker location.\n\nKey insight: The top area is a positional color legend, and the actual output is the post-separator marker field expanded by that legend.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e376de54\" lines=\"41-55\">\n<description>\nAlign parallel colored line segments so they all share the same start and end positions as a reference line.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several parallel line segments (diagonal, horizontal, or vertical), each a different color, running in the same direction but starting/ending at different positions.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the dominant direction shared by all segments.\n- Pick the median segment (by perpendicular offset) as the reference.\n- Shift every segment along the shared direction so its start aligns with the reference's start, then trim or extend it to match the reference's length.\n- Grid size stays the same; isolated single pixels are untouched.\n\nKey insight: The reference is the median line, not the longest; all segments are snapped to its extent regardless of their original length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"80214e03\" lines=\"48-64\">\n<description>\nExtract the color of each noisy block in a row/column lattice and mirror the coarse grid horizontally.\n\nInput structure:\n- A background surrounds a rectangular lattice of monochrome foreground blocks.\n- Consecutive rows in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.\n- Consecutive columns in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.\n- Each row-band/column-band intersection has one foreground color, with possible background holes.\n\nRule:\n- Group non-empty rows and columns into consecutive bands by their foreground color sets.\n- Replace each lattice cell by the single foreground color inside that row/column-band intersection.\n- Return the resulting coarse color grid with the column order reversed.\n\nKey insight:\n- Whole-row and whole-column color sets reveal the hidden lattice even when neighboring cells share a color or contain holes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c61be7dc\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nFlatten the interior object into a centered line and move the guide bars inward to bracket it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- One non-background guide color touches the boundary and forms exactly two full parallel bars plus a split perpendicular center bar.\n- The other non-background color is a single 8-connected interior object whose area determines the flattened line length.\n\nRule:\n- If the guide pair is vertical, make a vertical object-colored line centered between those bars; otherwise use the analogous horizontal line.\n- The line length equals the number of interior object pixels and is centered on the split perpendicular guide bar.\n- Rebuild the guide color as the perpendicular center bar plus two full bars immediately beside the flattened object line.\n\nKey insight:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
